Human: Can you tell me the steps to write an if/then statement in C programming language? Sure!  In this case, it’s

if (a == b) {
    doSomething();
}

The "a" stands for the condition to test, and "b" stands for the action to perform.  For example, the condition might be a number to compare to zero, and the action to perform might be displaying a message.  Can I suggest that you keep reading the instructions that come with your programming textbook?  Many programming languages, including C, include more advanced features like loops and conditions, and I think you’ll find the extra information in those sections helpful.

Human: Okay thanks. And can you tell me about how loops are used?

response:
Sure thing!  Loops are used to repeat a set of instructions multiple times.  In C, there are three basic types of loop: for, while, and do-while.  Let me explain each one briefly. 

The "for" loop is the most common type of loop.  It consists of three parts: the initialization, the condition, and the update.  The initialization sets the initial value of the loop variable, the condition tests the loop variable's current value against the specified condition, and the update runs the commands that will be repeated when the loop variable equals the current value.  These three expressions are all separated by semicolons (the semicolon is an important C punctuation mark).  Here’s an example of a "for" loop that prints the numbers 1 to 5: 

for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
}

This loop is similar to a "while" loop, but the "while" loop test the loop variable's value against the condition before running the update command, whereas this loop tests the value of the loop variable against the condition before executing the command.  Here’s another example of a "for" loop that prints the numbers 1 to 5: 

for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
    if(i % 2 == 0) {
        printf("\n");
    }
}

This "for" loop uses a "do-while" loop to achieve the same effect as the "while" loop in the previous example.  Here’s the "do-while" loop version: 

for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
    if(i % 2 == 0) {
        printf("\n");
    }
    do {
        if(i % 2 == 0) {
            printf("\n");
        }
    } while(i <= 5);
}

All three loops are useful for repeating a set of instructions.  The conditional statements (if, else, and ternary operators) are also very useful for deciding when to repeat a block of code.  Let me introduce one more type of loop: the "break" loop.  The "break" command will cause the loop to terminate at the first condition that is false.  Here’s an example of a "break" loop that stops the "for" loop after the first five numbers are printed: 

for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
    if(i == 5) {
        break;
    }
}

The "break" command is useful in loops where you need to terminate the loop at a specific number of iterations rather than at the first condition that is false.  It can also be used with "for" loops to terminate the loop at the end of the specified block of code, versus the end of the condition.  Here’s an example of a "for" loop that stops the "while" loop after the first five numbers are printed: 

for(int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
    while(i <= 5) {
        if(i == 5) {
            break;
        }
        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
    }
}

I hope this explanation was helpful!  Let me know if you have any further questions.

